Auto-Waiting (One of Playwright's biggest strengths)

Many beginners coming from Selenium think:
await page.waitForTimeout(5000);
is normal.

In Playwright, it's usually a bad practice.




What is Auto-Waiting?

When you write:
await page.getByRole('button', {
  name: 'Login'
}).click();

Playwright automatically waits for the button to:

Exist in DOM
Become visible
Become enabled
Become stable (not moving)
Then it clicks.

You don't need:
await page.waitForTimeout(5000);
before every action.

Example

Imagine page loads after 3 seconds.

await page.getByRole('button', {
  name: 'Login'
}).click();

Playwright will wait automatically and then click.

Auto-Wait in Assertions
await expect(
  page.getByText('Login Successful')
).toBeVisible();

Playwright keeps checking for a few seconds until:
Login Successful
appears.

If it appears → Pass ✅
If not → Fail ❌





Explicit Waits (When Needed)

Sometimes you do need waits.

Wait for URL
await page.waitForURL('**/dashboard');
Wait for Element
await page.locator('#result').waitFor();
Wait for Visibility
await page.locator('#result')
  .waitFor({ state: 'visible' });


Avoid This
await page.waitForTimeout(5000);

Interviewers often ask:

"Do you use waitForTimeout?"
Good answer:
Only for debugging. In automation scripts I prefer Playwright's auto-waiting and explicit waits like waitForURL() or locator().waitFor().


Interview Question
Why is Playwright more stable than Selenium?
One strong answer:
Playwright provides built-in auto-waiting for actions and assertions, reducing flaky tests and minimizing the need for hard-coded waits.